</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A6.F9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="A6.F9.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Differential observations for <em id="A6.F9.7.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Boxing</em> (the first subfigure), <em id="A6.F9.7.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Pong</em> (the second subfigure), and <em id="A6.F9.7.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Krull</em> tasks (the last subfigure). Each subfigure comprises three rows, and each illustrates a distinct form of observation. The first row displays the raw observations, the second row shows observations processed using vanilla frame differencing (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Dynamic Modulation ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ DyMoDreamer: World Modeling with Dynamic Modulation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and the third row presents the differential observations constructed using our proposed approach, which combines frame differencing with dilated convolutions.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
